40:       // Beyond the simple case, we say that one type is "always bit-castable" to another if:
41:       // - (1) `From` and `To` have the same value representation, and in addition every possible value of `From` has
42:       //   a corresponding value in the `To` type (in other words, the set of values of `To` is a superset of the set of
43:       //   values of `From`);
44:       // - (2) When the corresponding values are not the same value (as, for example, between an unsigned and a signed
45:       //   integer, where a large positive value of the unsigned integer corresponds to a negative value in the signed
46:       //   integer type), the value of `To` that results from a bitwise copy of `From` is the same what would be
47:       //   produced by the built-in assignment (if it were defined for the two types, to which there are minor
48:       //   exceptions, e.g. built-in arrays).
49:       //
50:       // In practice, that means:
51:       // - all integral types (except `bool`, see below) -- that is, character types and `int` types, both signed and
52:       //   unsigned...
53:       // - as well as arrays of such types...
54:       // - ...that have the same size.
55:       //
56:       // Other trivially-copyable types can't be validly bit-cast outside of their own type:
57:       // - floating-point types normally have different sizes and thus aren't bit-castable between each other (fails
58:       // #1);
